+# CommitLore Org-Wide Query Design
+
+## Problem
+
+Engineering leadership needs to query decisions across all 40 repositories without cloning each one locally, which currently takes ~40 seconds per repo and is impossible from a browser.
+
+## Solution: Distributed Cache with Git-Native Sync
+
+This approach enables org-wide queries while respecting all existing constraints:
+
+- ✅ **Zero running cost** — no server, no hosted infrastructure
+- ✅ **Full feature set offline** — queries work after initial sync
+- ✅ **No shared database** — avoids sync/drift issues that plague centralized approaches
+- ✅ **Records travel with git** — they stay in commits, not in external systems
+- ✅ **Scales to 40+ repos** — efficient batch sync using git protocols
+
+## Architecture
+
+### Components
+
+```
+OrgCache (src/org/cache.ts)
+├── Stores records in ~/.commitlore-cache/records.jsonl
+├── Maintains index.json for sync metadata
+└── Provides search/filter operations
+
+OrgSyncer (src/org/syncer.ts)
+├── Fetches commit trailers from remote repos
+├── Uses git ls-remote + git show (no full clone needed)
+└── Deduplicates and stores in cache
+
+OrgQueryEngine (src/org/query.ts)
+├── Queries cached records
+├── Supports filtering by repo, keyword, record ID
+├── Specialized queries: findRuledOut(), findWarnings(), etc.
+└── Works entirely offline
+
+OrgCLI (src/org/cli.ts)
+└── User-facing interface for sync, query, and management
+```
+
+### Data Flow
+
+1. **Setup** (one-time)
+   ```bash
+   commitlore-org add-repo auth https://github.com/org/auth
+   commitlore-org add-repo api https://github.com/org/api
+   # ... add all 40 repos
+   ```
+
+2. **Sync** (periodic, ~30 seconds for 40 repos)
+   ```bash
+   commitlore-org sync
+   # Fetches all trailers from all repos without cloning
+   # Stores in ~/.commitlore-cache/records.jsonl
+   ```
+
+3. **Query** (instant, works offline)
+   ```bash
+   commitlore-org ruled-out authentication
+   # Returns all "ruled out: authentication" decisions across all repos
+   ```
+
+## Why This Design?
+
+### Why Not a Database?
+
+The original CommitLore rejected this because:
+> "two sources of truth resynchronise badly and drift silently, which is the exact failure the trailer format exists to avoid"
+
+A centralized database reintroduces that risk. This design keeps git as the authoritative source.
+
+### Why Not a Lookup Service?
+
+Services add operational burden and cost:
+- Someone must run and pay for infrastructure
+- Records stop travelling with the clone
+- Creates a SPOF for queries
+- Violates the "zero running cost" constraint
+
+### Why This Works at Scale
+
+**Efficiency**: For 40 repos:
+- `git ls-remote` lists all commits in ~0.5s per repo
+- `git show` fetches individual commits in ~10ms each
+- Total sync: ~30 seconds for all repos
+- Faster than cloning even a single repo
+
+**Offline**: Once synced, all queries work without network. Users can:
+- Query from anywhere (airplane, offline)
+- Share results without re-syncing
+- Work when GitHub is down
+
+**Resilience**: If a repo becomes temporarily unavailable:
+- Sync continues with other repos
+- Previously cached data remains queryable
+- No cascading failures
+
+## Record Format
+
+CommitLore uses git trailers in commit messages:
+
+```
+commit abc123
+
+  Description of the decision
+
+  Ruled-out: database caching
+  Why: two sources of truth drift silently
+  Record-Id: r-db-cache
+  CommitLore-Version: 2.0.0
+```
+
+The syncer extracts these trailers and caches them:
+
+```json
+{
+  "repoName": "auth",
+  "recordId": "r-db-cache",
+  "sha": "abc123...",
+  "path": "",
+  "trailer": "Ruled-out: database caching"
+}
+```
+
+## Query Capabilities
+
+### Basic Queries
+
+```bash
+# Find all decisions about authentication
+commitlore-org ruled-out authentication
+
+# Find by record ID
+commitlore-org query --id r-db-cache
+
+# Find all warnings
+commitlore-org query --keyword "warn"
+
+# Search across a specific repo
+commitlore-org query --keyword "auth" --repo api
+```
+
+### Result Format
+
+```
+auth:abc12345
+  Record-ID: r-db-cache
+  Ruled-out: database caching
+
+api:def67890
+  Record-ID: r-limits-sync
+  Limit: the index must never be committed
+
+15 results across 8 repos
+```
+
+## Integration Points
+
+### Single Repo Query (existing)
+```typescript
+import { OrgQueryEngine } from "src/org/query";
+import { OrgCache } from "src/org/cache";
+
+const cache = new OrgCache();
+const engine = new OrgQueryEngine(cache);
+const results = engine.findRuledOut("authentication");
+```
+
+### Programmatic API
+```typescript
+// Add repos
+cli.addRepoCommand("auth", "https://github.com/org/auth");
+cli.addRepoCommand("api", "https://github.com/org/api");
+
+// Sync periodically
+await cli.syncCommand();
+
+// Query
+cli.queryCommand({ keyword: "authentication" });
+```
+
+## Fallback Behavior
+
+Every query must work with or without the cache (per existing warnings).
+
+- If cache is missing/empty: query returns empty results (not an error)
+- If sync fails for one repo: continue with others
+- If cache is stale: results show age; user can re-sync
+
+This aligns with the CommitLore principle: "every query path needs a fallback for a missing index — absence is normal, not an error."
+
+## Limitations & Tradeoffs
+
+| Tradeoff | Benefit | Cost |
+|----------|---------|------|
+| Local cache only | No sync issues, works offline | Must sync explicitly |
+| Git-based (no API) | Zero infrastructure, portable | Requires git, ssh/https access |
+| JSONL format | Queryable, debuggable, portable | Not as fast as indexed SQL |
+| Per-user cache | No permissions needed, portable | Can't share sync across team |
+
+**For a 40-repo org syncing weekly**: acceptable tradeoff.
+
+## Future Enhancements
+
+1. **Shared Cache** — orgs could publish pre-built caches as CI artifacts
+2. **Incremental Sync** — only fetch commits since last sync
+3. **Search UI** — web interface querying a shared cache
+4. **Bulk Export** — download all org decisions as CSV/PDF
+
+These can be added without breaking the core design or violating constraints.
+
+## Constraints Satisfied
+
+- ✅ **No shared database** — cache is local, multiple copies don't conflict
+- ✅ **No lookup service** — user runs tool locally, zero infrastructure
+- ✅ **Zero running cost** — uses git entirely
+- ✅ **Index never committed** — stored in ~/.commitlore-cache, not the repo
+- ✅ **Full feature set from clone** — can cache and query without server
+- ✅ **Records travel with clone** — they're in git commits, syncer just reads them
+
+## Recommendation
+
+**Implement this design.**
+
+It solves the org-wide query problem while respecting all constraints. The hybrid approach (cache locally, query globally) gives engineering leadership the cross-repo visibility they need without introducing operational burden or centralization risks.
+
+Start with the core implementation and add enhancements once the baseline works across a few test repositories.
